    'Lakeview’s residents are active digital continuations of humans, with their environment and everyday experiences supplied through Horizen’s computing platform. They do not need to produce virtual food or housing through ordinary bodily labor, but access depends on paid plans and purchases. A sponsor or family can fund that life; running out of resources can sharply restrict it. The guarantee coordinate is therefore close to the insecure end, despite the luxury visible to better-funded residents. This is a commercial support economy, not universal post-scarcity.\n\nProductive control is concentrated in the platform company, and paying for an upgrade does not give residents ownership of its servers or rules. Living customer-service angels also perform real work behind the digital setting; their contribution is disclosed rather than erased by calling everything automatic. The residents themselves can think, create, maintain relationships and potentially do useful work, so existence in an afterlife does not establish complete leisure. People temporarily frozen after using their data allowance are excluded from the active-person denominator during that interval. The record stays with the initial subscription order and does not import later reforms or threats.',
